.Dd September 8, 2026
.Dt BOSD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bosd
.Nd on-screen display engine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Bro Fl d | Fl C Brc
.Nm
.Op Fl ho
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Op Fl b Ar badge
.Op Fl s Ar scale
.Op Fl x Ar offset
.Op Fl y Ar offset
.Bro Ar icon | Fl c Ar countdown Brc
.Op Ar hold_seconds
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
paints a PNG glyph, scaled to the internal panel and outlined for
legibility, in a centered click-through ARGB32 overlay window.
It is the display engine behind media-key and hotkey feedback: a
warm daemon owns the window, and clients replace the visible glyph
in place, so rapid key repeats never flash.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-n instance"
.It Fl b Ar badge
Draw
.Ar badge
as a superscript at the glyph's upper right \(em white with a black
outline, about a quarter of the glyph height.
Used for index digits and short labels
.Pq e.g. a headphone number, Dq All .
At most 31 characters; whitespace is not permitted.
.It Fl C
Clear the channel's active render \(em hide an in-progress show,
such as a long countdown \(em and return immediately.
Without a running daemon there is nothing to clear.
.It Fl c Ar countdown
Count down from
.Ar countdown
to 1 in huge outlined digits on a full-panel overlay, one second
per digit
.Pq Ar hold_seconds overrides ,
then exit \(em the screenshot timer.
Replaces
.Ar icon
and is dispatched to the channel daemon like any show, replacing
whatever it displays (and vice versa); without a daemon the
invoking process draws it and returns when done.
Honors
.Fl b , o , s , x ,
and
.Fl y :
the badge rides the digits' upper right and
.Fl s
scales the digits.
.It Fl d
Run as the display daemon for
.Ar instance ,
listening on a per-user datagram socket.
.It Fl h
Print usage and exit.
.It Fl n Ar instance
Select the OSD channel (default
.Dq Li default ) .
Independent channels own independent windows and sockets.
.It Fl o
Skip the black outline normally drawn behind the glyph for
legibility on any wallpaper.
Suited to full-color artwork that carries its own contrast.
.It Fl s Ar scale
Multiply the panel-derived render size by
.Ar scale
(0.1 to 8.0, default 1.0).
The glyph is normally sized from the panel height, independent of
the PNG's native resolution;
.Fl s Li 2.0
renders it twice that size.
.It Fl x Ar offset
Shift the artwork horizontally by
.Ar offset
pixels from panel center: positive right, negative left.
Placement semantics are those of
.Fl y .
.It Fl y Ar offset
Shift the artwork vertically by
.Ar offset
pixels from panel center: positive down, negative up
.Pq X11 coordinate sense .
Consumers sharing a screen with fixed chrome (e.g. a taskbar) can
recenter within the remaining space, such as
.Fl y Li -20
above a 40-pixel bottom bar.
Offset placement is exact; artwork carried past a panel edge is
clipped at the edge rather than shown on a neighboring output,
so a large offset slides a show in from beyond it.
Only offset-free shows are clamped to remain fully visible.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl d ,
.Nm
sends a show request to the running daemon for the channel; if no
daemon is alive, it paints the glyph itself and exits when
.Ar hold_seconds
(default 2.0, clamped to 0.01\(en30) elapse.
.Sh ICON RESOLUTION
An
.Ar icon
containing a slash is used as a path.
A bare name is searched in each directory of the colon-separated
.Ev BOSD_PATH ,
then in the compiled share directory.
A
.Pa .png
suffix is appended when missing.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "BOSD_PATH"
.It Ev BOSD_PATH
Colon-separated icon search path, tried before the compiled default.
.It Ev DISPLAY
X11 display to draw on.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/bosd.instance.uid.sock" -compact
.It Pa /tmp/bosd. Ns Ar instance . Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .sock
show-request socket
.It Pa /tmp/bosd. Ns Ar instance . Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .pid
daemon pid
.It Pa /usr/local/share/bosd
default icon directory
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Warm the airplane-mode channel at session start:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane -d &
.Ed
.Pp
Flash the on glyph for 1.5 seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane airplane-on 1.5
.Ed
.Pp
Show the second headphone output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n audio -b 2 audio-headphones 1.5
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xrandr 1
